Protocol Reference: File Transfer Protocol (FTP)
Overview
FTP (File Transfer Protocol) is a standard network protocol used to transfer files between a client and a server over a TCP/IP connection.

Port Allocation:

Port 21: Control channel (receives commands, authenticates users).

Port 20: Data channel (transfers actual file contents).

Security Risk: Plaintext protocol. All credentials, commands, and transferred files are transmitted without encryption and can be sniffed via MiTM (Man-in-the-Middle) attacks.

Secure Alternatives
SFTP (SSH File Transfer Protocol): Runs over SSH (Port 22). Encrypts both credentials and data traffic.

FTPS (FTP over SSL/TLS): Standard FTP wrapped in an explicit or implicit TLS/SSL encryption layer.

Key FTP Status Codes
220: Service ready for new user.

230: User logged in, proceed (Login successful).

331: User name okay, need password.

430 / 530: Invalid credentials or authorization failure.

Command Cheat Sheet
Network Reconnaissance
Bash
# Test connectivity to the target
ping <TARGET_IP>

# Scan for open ports and service versions
nmap -sV <TARGET_IP>

FTP Operations
Bash
# Connect to FTP server
ftp <TARGET_IP>

# Authenticate anonymously (when prompted)
Name: anonymous
Password: <Leave blank / Press Enter>

# Interacting with FTP prompt
ftp> help           # Displays available FTP client commands
ftp> ls             # Lists files in the current remote directory
ftp> status         # Displays current connection status
ftp> get flag.txt   # Downloads 'flag.txt' to local directory
ftp> exit           # Closes connection and exits client
